window.IND_FEED_BODY = window.IND_FEED_BODY || {};
Object.assign(window.IND_FEED_BODY, {"ss-ds.samudra-manthan-7":{"src":"story:ds.samudra-manthan:scenes:7:text","title":"The Day Both Sides Pulled the Same Rope · 8 of 9","body":"And at the very end, the amrita. And both sides, who had pulled the same rope for an age, immediately began to fight over it — which is a story for another page, and not a flattering one for anybody.","art":"art/story/sm/ds-samudra-manthan.jpg","route":"#/story/ds.samudra-manthan|s7","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Day Both Sides Pulled the Same Rope","facts":[["From","Devas & Asuras"],["Read","5 min"]]},"ss-ds.prahlada-1":{"src":"story:ds.prahlada:scenes:1:text","title":"The Boy Who Would Not Change His Mind · 2 of 9","body":"And his own son said it. Not to be difficult. Prahlada simply loved Vishnu, from very small, the way some children love something before anybody teaches them to — and when he was asked about it he told the truth, because he had not yet learned that truth is sometimes dangerous.","art":"art/story/sm/ds-prahlada.jpg","route":"#/story/ds.prahlada|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Boy Who Would Not Change His Mind","facts":[["From","Devas & Asuras"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-ds.prahlada-2":{"src":"story:ds.prahlada:scenes:2:text","title":"The Boy Who Would Not Change His Mind · 3 of 9","body":"His father sent him to the best teachers in the world — Shukracharya’s own sons — to have it schooled out of him. Prahlada listened politely to every lesson, learned it all, and went on saying the name.","art":"art/story/sm/ds-prahlada.jpg","route":"#/story/ds.prahlada|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Boy Who Would Not Change His Mind","facts":[["From","Devas & Asuras"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-ds.prahlada-3":{"src":"story:ds.prahlada:scenes:3:text","title":"The Boy Who Would Not Change His Mind · 4 of 9","body":"\"I am the strongest being alive,\" his father said. \"I have beaten everyone. Why will you not simply say MY name?\"","art":"art/story/sm/ds-prahlada.jpg","route":"#/story/ds.prahlada|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Boy Who Would Not Change His Mind","facts":[["From","Devas & Asuras"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-ds.prahlada-5":{"src":"story:ds.prahlada:scenes:5:text","title":"The Boy Who Would Not Change His Mind · 6 of 9","body":"The stories say his father tried, over and over, to frighten him out of it, and every time the boy came through calm, and every time the calm was more infuriating than the disobedience. Nothing was working, and the not-working was the worst part.","art":"art/story/sm/ds-prahlada.jpg","route":"#/story/ds.prahlada|s5","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Boy Who Would Not Change His Mind","facts":[["From","Devas & Asuras"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-ds.prahlada-6":{"src":"story:ds.prahlada:scenes:6:text","title":"The Boy Who Would Not Change His Mind · 7 of 9","body":"How that ends — with a pillar, and a form that is neither man nor animal, at an hour that is neither day nor night — is told in The Ten Avatars, under Narasimha. What matters here is what happened afterwards.","art":"art/story/sm/ds-prahlada.jpg","route":"#/story/ds.prahlada|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Boy Who Would Not Change His Mind","facts":[["From","Devas & Asuras"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-ds.prahlada-7":{"src":"story:ds.prahlada:scenes:7:text","title":"The Boy Who Would Not Change His Mind · 8 of 9","body":"When the terrible lion-form had done what it came for and nobody could go near it, it was the boy who walked up to it. Not afraid. And the stories say it was Prahlada’s calm, and only that, which settled it.","art":"art/story/sm/ds-prahlada.jpg","route":"#/story/ds.prahlada|s7","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Boy Who Would Not Change His Mind","facts":[["From","Devas & Asuras"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-ds.tarakasura-1":{"src":"story:ds.tarakasura:scenes:1:text","title":"Only a Child Not Yet Born · 2 of 8","body":"That is a clever wish. It does not say \"nobody can beat me\" — a wish like that gets refused. It names one person who does not exist and is not going to.","art":"art/story/sm/ds-tarakasura.jpg","route":"#/story/ds.tarakasura|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Only a Child Not Yet Born","facts":[["From","Devas & Asuras"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-ds.tarakasura-2":{"src":"story:ds.tarakasura:scenes:2:text","title":"Only a Child Not Yet Born · 3 of 8","body":"So Tarakasura took the three worlds, and the devas went to look for the only door there was, and found it shut. Shiva was deep in meditation and had been for an age.","art":"art/story/sm/ds-tarakasura.jpg","route":"#/story/ds.tarakasura|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Only a Child Not Yet Born","facts":[["From","Devas & Asuras"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-ds.tarakasura-3":{"src":"story:ds.tarakasura:scenes:3:text","title":"Only a Child Not Yet Born · 4 of 8","body":"It was Parvati who changed that, and not by being sent. She chose him, and she went to the mountains herself, and she waited — through cold and heat and years of it — with a patience the stories admire more than they admire anybody’s strength in this whole shelf.","art":"art/story/sm/ds-tarakasura.jpg","route":"#/story/ds.tarakasura|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Only a Child Not Yet Born","facts":[["From","Devas & Asuras"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-ds.tarakasura-5":{"src":"story:ds.tarakasura:scenes:5:text","title":"Only a Child Not Yet Born · 6 of 8","body":"They made him commander of the armies of the devas, and gave him the vel — a spear — and he rode out on a peacock and ended what nobody older had been able to touch.","art":"art/story/sm/ds-tarakasura.jpg","route":"#/story/ds.tarakasura|s5","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Only a Child Not Yet Born","facts":[["From","Devas & Asuras"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-ds.tarakasura-6":{"src":"story:ds.tarakasura:scenes:6:text","title":"Only a Child Not Yet Born · 7 of 8","body":"In the south he is Murugan, and he is not a minor figure there at all — he is one of the most loved of all. Six great temples in Tamil Nadu, the Arupadai Veedu, are his houses, and people walk to them carrying kavadi. Skanda, Subramanya, Shanmukha, Kartikeya, Murugan: all the same young commander.","art":"art/story/sm/ds-tarakasura.jpg","route":"#/story/ds.tarakasura|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Only a Child Not Yet Born","facts":[["From","Devas & Asuras"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-ds.shukracharya-1":{"src":"story:ds.shukracharya:scenes:1:text","title":"The Teacher Who Could Bring Anyone Back · 2 of 8","body":"Which made a war unwinnable. Devas fell and stayed fallen. Asuras fell and got up in the morning. The devas could win every single day and be no further forward.","art":"art/story/sm/ds-shukracharya.jpg","route":"#/story/ds.shukracharya|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Teacher Who Could Bring Anyone Back","facts":[["From","Devas & Asuras"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-ds.shukracharya-2":{"src":"story:ds.shukracharya:scenes:2:text","title":"The Teacher Who Could Bring Anyone Back · 3 of 8","body":"So the devas did something interesting. They did not steal it and they did not attack him. They sent him a student — Kacha, the son of their own guru — to ask to be taught.","art":"art/story/sm/ds-shukracharya.jpg","route":"#/story/ds.shukracharya|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Teacher Who Could Bring Anyone Back","facts":[["From","Devas & Asuras"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-ds.shukracharya-3":{"src":"story:ds.shukracharya:scenes:3:text","title":"The Teacher Who Could Bring Anyone Back · 4 of 8","body":"And Shukracharya, knowing perfectly well whose son had turned up at his door, took him in and taught him. Because he was a teacher, and a student had asked. The stories are quite clear that he knew.","art":"art/story/sm/ds-shukracharya.jpg","route":"#/story/ds.shukracharya|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Teacher Who Could Bring Anyone Back","facts":[["From","Devas & Asuras"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-ds.shukracharya-5":{"src":"story:ds.shukracharya:scenes:5:text","title":"The Teacher Who Could Bring Anyone Back · 6 of 8","body":"The last time, they burned him and mixed the ashes into Shukracharya’s own drink. So when the guru called Kacha back, the answer came from inside him — and to let his student live, he had to teach him the vidya then and there, and let the boy bring HIM back afterwards.","art":"art/story/sm/ds-shukracharya.jpg","route":"#/story/ds.shukracharya|s5","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Teacher Who Could Bring Anyone Back","facts":[["From","Devas & Asuras"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-ds.shukracharya-6":{"src":"story:ds.shukracharya:scenes:6:text","title":"The Teacher Who Could Bring Anyone Back · 7 of 8","body":"Which is the moment the knowledge left the asuras for good. Not stolen. Given away, by a teacher, in the only order that would let his student walk out alive.","art":"art/story/sm/ds-shukracharya.jpg","route":"#/story/ds.shukracharya|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Teacher Who Could Bring Anyone Back","facts":[["From","Devas & Asuras"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-ds.bhasmasura-1":{"src":"story:ds.bhasmasura:scenes:1:text","title":"The Gift He Wanted to Try Out · 2 of 8","body":"\"Let anything I put my hand on turn to ash.\"","art":"art/story/sm/ds-bhasmasura.jpg","route":"#/story/ds.bhasmasura|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Gift He Wanted to Try Out","facts":[["From","Devas & Asuras"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-ds.bhasmasura-2":{"src":"story:ds.bhasmasura:scenes:2:text","title":"The Gift He Wanted to Try Out · 3 of 8","body":"And Shiva said yes. He had said he would, and he does not go back on it. The stories treat this as a real weakness of his and do not tidy it away — he gives, and then has to live with what he has given.","art":"art/story/sm/ds-bhasmasura.jpg","route":"#/story/ds.bhasmasura|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Gift He Wanted to Try Out","facts":[["From","Devas & Asuras"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-ds.bhasmasura-3":{"src":"story:ds.bhasmasura:scenes:3:text","title":"The Gift He Wanted to Try Out · 4 of 8","body":"And the very first thought Bhasmasura had — the very first — was to try it on Shiva. On the person who had just given it to him. So Shiva ran, which is not a sentence you often get to read, and Bhasmasura came after him.","art":"art/story/sm/ds-bhasmasura.jpg","route":"#/story/ds.bhasmasura|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Gift He Wanted to Try Out","facts":[["From","Devas & Asuras"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-ds.bhasmasura-5":{"src":"story:ds.bhasmasura:scenes:5:text","title":"The Gift He Wanted to Try Out · 6 of 8","body":"Mohini danced, and Bhasmasura — who could not bear to be less impressive than anybody — copied every movement exactly. Turn for turn. Step for step. And Mohini, mid-dance, quite naturally, placed a hand on top of her own head.","art":"art/story/sm/ds-bhasmasura.jpg","route":"#/story/ds.bhasmasura|s5","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Gift He Wanted to Try Out","facts":[["From","Devas & Asuras"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-ds.bhasmasura-6":{"src":"story:ds.bhasmasura:scenes:6:text","title":"The Gift He Wanted to Try Out · 7 of 8","body":"And so did he. And that was the end of Bhasmasura, undone by nothing whatsoever except being unable to let someone else look better than him.","art":"art/story/sm/ds-bhasmasura.jpg","route":"#/story/ds.bhasmasura|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Gift He Wanted to Try Out","facts":[["From","Devas & Asuras"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-ds.banasura-1":{"src":"story:ds.banasura:scenes:1:text","title":"The Asura Who Guarded His Daughter Too Well · 2 of 8","body":"Shiva was so pleased that he came and stood guard at Banasura’s own gate. Which meant Banasura’s fortress could not be entered by anybody at all, and Banasura — who was a very good drummer and a less good listener — began to find this boring, and complained that he had nobody left to fight.","art":"art/story/sm/ds-banasura.jpg","route":"#/story/ds.banasura|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Asura Who Guarded His Daughter Too Well","facts":[["From","Devas & Asuras"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-ds.banasura-2":{"src":"story:ds.banasura:scenes:2:text","title":"The Asura Who Guarded His Daughter Too Well · 3 of 8","body":"He also had a daughter, Usha. And he kept her behind all of it — the thousand arms, the fortress, the god at the gate — because he had been warned about her future and had decided the answer was walls.","art":"art/story/sm/ds-banasura.jpg","route":"#/story/ds.banasura|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Asura Who Guarded His Daughter Too Well","facts":[["From","Devas & Asuras"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-ds.banasura-3":{"src":"story:ds.banasura:scenes:3:text","title":"The Asura Who Guarded His Daughter Too Well · 4 of 8","body":"Usha dreamed of a young man she had never met. Her friend Chitralekha, who could draw anybody, drew portraits until Usha pointed at one: Aniruddha, Krishna’s own grandson. And Chitralekha, who had other talents too, went and fetched him.","art":"art/story/sm/ds-banasura.jpg","route":"#/story/ds.banasura|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Asura Who Guarded His Daughter Too Well","facts":[["From","Devas & Asuras"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-ds.banasura-5":{"src":"story:ds.banasura:scenes:5:text","title":"The Asura Who Guarded His Daughter Too Well · 6 of 8","body":"So the story arrives somewhere genuinely uncomfortable: Krishna on one side, Shiva on the other, both keeping their word, and neither of them wrong. It is not a good-against-evil fight. It is two promises meeting.","art":"art/story/sm/ds-banasura.jpg","route":"#/story/ds.banasura|s5","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Asura Who Guarded His Daughter Too Well","facts":[["From","Devas & Asuras"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-ds.banasura-6":{"src":"story:ds.banasura:scenes:6:text","title":"The Asura Who Guarded His Daughter Too Well · 7 of 8","body":"It ended without Banasura being destroyed. Shiva asked for his devotee’s life and it was granted; Banasura kept a few of his thousand arms and went back to drumming; and Usha and Aniruddha were married, which is how these things are settled in the end anyway.","art":"art/story/sm/ds-banasura.jpg","route":"#/story/ds.banasura|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Asura Who Guarded His Daughter Too Well","facts":[["From","Devas & Asuras"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-ds.andhaka-gift-1":{"src":"story:ds.andhaka-gift:scenes:1:text","title":"The Asura Who Asked to Be Remembered · 2 of 6","body":"There is a pattern in the Shiva stories. An asura fights him — Andhaka, Gajasura, Jalandhara, and others depending on which telling you have — and loses. And then, at the end, the losing one is granted a last request. Not spared. Granted something.","art":"art/story/sm/ds-andhaka-gift.jpg","route":"#/story/ds.andhaka-gift|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Asura Who Asked to Be Remembered","facts":[["From","Devas & Asuras"],["Read","3 min"]]},"ss-ds.andhaka-gift-2":{"src":"story:ds.andhaka-gift:scenes:2:text","title":"The Asura Who Asked to Be Remembered · 3 of 6","body":"And over and over the request is the same sort of thing. Let me be near you. Let people remember me when they remember you. Let something of mine be part of how you are worshipped.","art":"art/story/sm/ds-andhaka-gift.jpg","route":"#/story/ds.andhaka-gift|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Asura Who Asked to Be Remembered","facts":[["From","Devas & Asuras"],["Read","3 min"]]},"ss-ds.andhaka-gift-4":{"src":"story:ds.andhaka-gift:scenes:4:text","title":"The Asura Who Asked to Be Remembered · 5 of 6","body":"Compare that with how most stories in the world end for the one who lost. Here the loser gets asked what he wants. That is not softness. It is a whole opinion about what an enemy is.","art":"art/story/sm/ds-andhaka-gift.jpg","route":"#/story/ds.andhaka-gift|s4","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Asura Who Asked to Be Remembered","facts":[["From","Devas & Asuras"],["Read","3 min"]]},"en-mahabharata-32-1":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|32","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":32},"src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:31:cards:1:text","title":"The Mahabharata · Gandhari Counts","body":"They had been in the city for eighteen days, hearing drums. Now they are outside, in the grey light, looking for particular people among a great many.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Mahabharata, night 32","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","32 of 33"]]},"en-mahabharata-32-2":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|32","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":32},"src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:31:cards:2:text","title":"The Mahabharata · Gandhari Counts","body":"Gandhari walked out with the cloth still over her eyes, holding somebody's arm, and she went across that field and found her sons. All of them. One after another, in order, the way you would count something.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Mahabharata, night 32","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","32 of 33"]]},"en-mahabharata-32-3":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|32","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":32},"src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:31:cards:3:text","title":"The Mahabharata · Gandhari Counts","body":"A hundred. She had made a hundred choices about names. She had told a hundred children not to run in the corridor. She had been warned, once, in a room, on a morning when it could still have been stopped, and she had said the warning out loud herself, and been overruled by a man who could not say no.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Mahabharata, night 32","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","32 of 33"]]},"en-mahabharata-32-4":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|32","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":32},"src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:31:cards:4:text","title":"The Mahabharata · Gandhari Counts","body":"And then she turned round to the one person on that field who could have prevented all of it.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Mahabharata, night 32","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","32 of 33"]]},"en-mahabharata-32-5":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|32","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":32},"src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:31:cards:5:text","title":"The Mahabharata · Gandhari Counts","body":"\"You could have stopped this,\" she said to Krishna. \"Not at the end. At any hour of any one of those eighteen days, and before that, in the hall, and before that, at the dice. You are the only person here who could have, and you did not.\"","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Mahabharata, night 32","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","32 of 33"]]},"en-mahabharata-32-7":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|32","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":32},"src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:31:cards:7:text","title":"The Mahabharata · Gandhari Counts","body":"Then Kunti walked over to her own five sons, who had won, and who were standing about not knowing what to do with their hands, and told them who Karna was.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Mahabharata, night 32","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","32 of 33"]]},"en-mahabharata-32-8":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|32","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":32},"src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:31:cards:8:text","title":"The Mahabharata · Gandhari Counts","body":"Yudhishthira did not shout either. He asked her when she had known, and she said, since before he was born. He asked why she had not said, and there was no answer that could be given standing on that field.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Mahabharata, night 32","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","32 of 33"]]},"en-mahabharata-32-9":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|32","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":32},"src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:31:cards:9:text","title":"The Mahabharata · Gandhari Counts","body":"\"Then he was my elder brother,\" he said. \"And I have spent my life at war with my brother. And you watched, and you said nothing, for forty years.\" And then, because there was nothing left to do with any of it, he lost his temper and laid a curse on all women — that no woman should ever be able to keep a secret again.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Mahabharata, night 32","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","32 of 33"]]},"en-mahabharata-32-10":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|32","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":32},"src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:31:cards:10:text","title":"The Mahabharata · Gandhari Counts","body":"It is a small and bitter and completely unfair thing to say, and the poem writes it down exactly as it happened and does not defend it. That is what this book does. It writes down what people actually say on the worst morning of their lives.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Mahabharata, night 32","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","32 of 33"]]},"en-mahabharata-32-11":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|32","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":32},"src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:31:cards:11:text","title":"The Mahabharata · Gandhari Counts","body":"And then the two mothers — the blindfolded one who had lost a hundred, and the one who had lost one and could never say so — went to the water together with the rest of the women, and did what has to be done for the dead, side by side, for everybody, without sorting them into sides.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Mahabharata, night 32","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","32 of 33"]]}});
